  "next": {
    "_comment": "Next release (design §3, PR 6): who may subscribe, on the MCP Events wire and through a tool. Loaders that predate it ignore this key. Under the next release, chat.message in subscribe and poll above is defined with public: true (otherwise it's tools-only and those cases are refused); each case above still holds unchanged. Two more events: crawl.finished, tools-only (declares neither personal, authorize nor public), arguments {job: string, required}, payload {pages: number}; deploy.finished, with an authorize that allows only user u1, arguments {env: string, required}, payload {status: string}. user is what identify returns (null: no user). Every refusal is answered by the SDK itself: Wakeshift isn't asked (no request to /events/subscribe or /events/poll), and the error is the dialect's forbidden code, its message from event-tools.json, data null. A draft client is any client name (some-draft-client); chatgpt is ChatGPT's dialect, as in errors.json rpc.",
    "_c1_dependent": "The core refuses the same requests (C1) for events whose manifest says subscribe: \"tools\", so an old SDK instance can't reopen them; these SDK fixtures check only the SDK's own refusal and that it never asks the core. Without C1 deployed, the SDK's refusal still holds.",
    "events_list": [
      "chat.message",
      "deploy.finished"
    ],
    "_events_list_comment": "events/list names only events a native client may subscribe to; crawl.finished (tools-only) is absent.",
    "precedence": [
      "Mcp-Name header mismatch",
      "unknown event (not found)",
      "tools-only (forbidden, native_tools_only; events/poll native_tools_only_poll)",
      "delivery mode (unsupported)",
      "arguments (invalid params)",
      "no user (forbidden, native_no_user)",
      "authorize (forbidden, native_refused)"
    ],
    "_precedence_comment": "The order an SDK checks events/subscribe and events/poll in; events/unsubscribe skips tools-only, no-delivery-mode and authorize checks.",
